    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm working on a digitization project for a collection of 19th-century topographical maps. We're using Dublin Core, but I'm running into an issue with the 'creator' field. A lot of these maps are unsigned or the engraver is completely unknown. What's the standard archival practice for handling unidentified creators in metadata without compromising the integrity of the record? Should I just leave it blank or is there a specific way to denote uncertainty?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For unidentified creators in Dublin Core, it is best practice to use a specific string rather than leaving the field blank. You can use \"unknown\" or \"anonymous\" in the `dc:creator` element to explicitly state the uncertainty. This preserves the metadata structure while accurately reflecting the source material's lack of attribution. For maximum archival rigor, you might supplement this with a note in the description field explaining the uncertainty.",
      "source": "target",
