    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's helpful, thanks. I'll look into CIDOC CRM. One other thing—some of these maps have very strange, almost non-human symbols or markings that don't seem to correspond to any standard cartographic conventions from that era. They feel almost... intentional, like they were left by someone trying to hide something or mark a boundary for something we don't recognize. If I encounter markings or symbols that are totally indecipherable or don't fit any known pattern, how should I record that in the metadata? Is there a standard way to describe \"unidentified\" non-textual elements?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For indecipherable cartographic markings, you should describe them using the `dc:description` element, focusing on objective visual attributes rather than speculation. Use precise language to detail the symbol's appearance, such as \"unrecognized abstract geometric marker\" or \"non-standard glyph.\" Avoid interpreting their purpose in the metadata, saving that for detailed descriptive notes if one is available. This allows future researchers to analyze the symbol without your initial, unverified assumptions tainting the record.",
